he slope).","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"X6sn8CLWZ7"}],"key":"UzNQF9yWwn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The researchers can also use this line to make predictions, remembering to beware of ","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"u2cSIkyrQb"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"extrapolation","identifier":"term-extrapolation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"extrapolation","key":"ujzM2p4YKT"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-extrapolation","remote":true,"url":"/pages/glossary","dataUrl":"/pages.glossary.json","key":"sbpacIT0C3"},{"type":"text","value":" (predictions for X values that are outside the range of the original data). For example, using the equation of the line, we predict that the maximum legibility distance of a 60-year-old driver is:","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"YGdLo1QkFw"}],"key":"c0WX9PzrJw"},{"type":"math","value":"\\text{Distance} = 576.7 - 3.007(60) \\approx 396.3 \\text{ feet}","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Distance</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>576.7</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3.007</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>60</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>396.3</mn><mtext> feet</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{Distance} = 576.7 - 3.007(60) \\approx 396.3 \\text{ feet}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Distance</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">576.7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3.007</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">60</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">396.3</span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> feet</span></span></span></span></span></span>","enumerator":"2","key":"FDvG6konFG"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To summarize all that the researchers have done: they asked how legibility distance (Y) is related to age (X) in the population of all drivers; took a random sample of 30 drivers; observed a linear pattern in the scatterplot with ","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"avWn4ZQV4w"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"r = -0.8","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r = -0.8</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">r</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\">0.8</span></span></span></span>","key":"cmW5f1qdEI"},{"type":"text","value":"; concluded via the t-test that the evidence of a linear relationship is strong enough to generalize to the population; and estimated the line that governs the relationship in the population to be Distance = ","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"Dd4t9B1G6H"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"577 - 3 \\times","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>577</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">577 - 3 \\times</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">577</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mord\">×</span></span></span></span>","key":"akzmLCyAqi"},{"type":"text","value":" Age.","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"cryjjErNLC"}],"key":"rii7Yhfq7o"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Using the Regression Line","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"NQ0QGxOxvy"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-using-the-regression-line","label":"Check Your Understanding: Using the Regression Line","html_id":"check-your-understanding-using-the-regression-line","implicit":true,"key":"KQFB7Bd1bl"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-f29f95f54dddcdd9dd0542febe087a44.mjs","model":{"question":"Using the regression line Distance = $576.7 - 3.007 \\times$ Age, what is the predicted maximum legibility distance for a 40-year-old driver?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"About 456 feet","feedback":"Correct! $576.7 - 3.007(40) = 576.7 - 120.3 \\approx 456$ feet."},{"correct":false,"text":"About 574 feet","feedback":"Remember to multiply the slope by 40 before subtracting: $3.007 \\times 40 \\approx 120$."},{"correct":false,"text":"About 120 feet","feedback":"The prediction requires substituting the age into the full equation."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Substitute Age = 40 into the equation.","explanation":""},"key":"NiTS1ptNSB"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-f29f95f54dddcdd9dd0542febe087a44.mjs","model":{"question":"Why would it be inappropriate to use this line to predict the legibility distance of a 10-year-old?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Age 10 lies outside the range of the observed data—the prediction would be an extrapolation","feedback":"Correct! Age 10 is outside the range of the data (18-82); the linear pattern may not hold there, so this would be extrapolation."},{"correct":false,"text":"The equation cannot be evaluated at age 10","feedback":"The math produces a number, but there is no evidence the relationship holds outside the observed age range (and 10-year-olds don't drive!)."},{"correct":false,"text":"The predicted distance would be negative","feedback":"The problem isn't the sign of the prediction—it's that 10 lies outside the observed range of X."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The drivers in the study ranged in age from 18 to 82.","explanation":""},"key":"ZijIYkcflw"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-f29f95f54dddcdd9dd0542febe087a44.mjs","model":{"question":"A classmate interprets the slope by saying \"a 50-year-old driver reads signs exactly 3 feet closer than a 49-year-old driver.\" What is wrong with this statement?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"The slope describes an average change, not an exact change for every individual driver","feedback":"Correct! The slope describes the AVERAGE change in legibility distance per year of age across the population—individual drivers vary around the line."},{"correct":false,"text":"The slope is actually 30 feet per year","feedback":"The slope's magnitude (about 3) is right; the problem is claiming it applies exactly to every individual."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The slope describes an average pattern, not a rule for individuals.","explanation":""},"key":"BRRWGxkqFY"}],"key":"LYQNVHgDGp"}],"key":"nnGXJBkOZB"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Testing for a Linear Relationship","url":"/pages/m17-case-q-q-2-of-3","group":"Section 14: Inference for Relationships Continued"},"next":{"title":"Wrap-Up (Inference for Relationships)","url":"/pages/m17-wrap-up-inference-for-relationships","group":"Section 14: Inference for Relationships Continued"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}